      "n_contexts": 12,
      "answer": "The context does not contain enough information to trace a complete regulatory path from the FAA to the maintenance crew servicing the CFM LEAP-1B engine.\n\nWhat the context does establish:\n\n- **FAA \u2192 ADs:** 14 CFR Part 39 establishes requirements for correcting unsafe conditions in aircraft, engines, propellers, and appliances (\u00a739.1), and FAA airworthiness directives are legally enforceable rules (\u00a739.3). FAA issues an AD when an unsafe condition exists and is likely to exist or develop in other products of the same type design (\u00a739.5).\n- **ADs \u2192 737 MAX:** FAA AD 2024-01-02 requires MCAS Software v2.0 installation within 90 days of its February 15, 2024 effective date, plus FCOM revision, AOA sensor inspection, and compliance reporting. It supersedes AD 2022-03-07, which superseded AD 2020-05-11.\n- **ADs \u2192 engine mount maintenance:** The 737 MAX CMM (Chapter 71, rev12) lists FAA AD 2024-01-02 and FAA AD 2022-03-07 among ADs applying to LEAP-1B engine mount inspection, and its revision history states rev12 updated inspection intervals per AD 2024-01-02 compliance.\n- **Operator-level compliance:** The G-ABCD compliance report shows an operator tracking AD 2024-01-02 (MCAS v1.7.3 installed, v2.0 due May 15, 2024, scheduled April 22-23, 2024 with BA Engineering), and the G-ABCD inspection report shows CMM-referenced engine mount corrosion findings handled by SR Technics.\n\nHowever, the context does not describe an explicit chain linking FAA AD issuance through the CMM to a specific maintenance crew's servicing actions for the CFM LEAP-1B engine. The CMM's listing of applicable ADs and the inspection report's CMM-referenced finding are separately stated facts; the context does not explicitly state that the CMM revision or the specific engine mount servicing was directed by those ADs beyond the CMM's own revision-history statement.",
