      "answer": "The context does not contain enough information to explain a \"full compliance chain from FAA-AD-2024 through to Southwest Airlines' 737 MAX fleet\" as a connected chain. What the evidence separately states:\n\n- **FAA AD 2024-01-02** (effective February 15, 2024) supersedes AD 2022-03-07 and requires, within 90 days, installation of MCAS Software v2.0 or later per Boeing Service Bulletin [SSN_REDACTED], Rev 2, dated January 8, 2024, plus FCOM revision, AOA sensor inspection, and compliance reporting.\n- **Boeing's MCAS Software v2.0 change record** (D6-87700-100) states v2.0 supersedes v1.7.3 and is \"required for compliance with FAA AD 2024-01-02.\"\n- **Southwest's fleet registry** (dated January 1, 2024) lists FAA AD 2024-01-02 as \"COMPLIANCE DUE - deadline May 15, 2024,\" with MCAS v1.7.3 on all 87 MAX 8 aircraft and updates scheduled February-May 2024 (phased, ~10 aircraft/month), target MCAS v2.0 per Boeing SB [SSN_REDACTED], Rev 2.\n\nThe context does not state that Southwest's updates were completed, nor does it document any verified compliance linkage beyond the registry's planned schedule. No causal or completed compliance chain is explicitly established in the evidence.",
